<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livonian Chronicle of Henry</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1227</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Chronicle of Henry of Livonia, Heinrici Chronicon Livoniae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Livonian Chronicle of Henry is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> chronicle of the conquest and conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> by German crusaders, written around 1225–1227 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia</a>, a priest who took part in the events. Covering the years from about 1180 to 1227, it is the main written source for the pre-Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonians">Livonians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a>, their lands, wars and beliefs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonian-chronicle-of-henry.jpg" alt="Livonian Chronicle of Henry" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown, copy of Henry of Livonia manuscript · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henriku_kroonika_papp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
